<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en-GB">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8"/>
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"/>
<title>The Island That Didn't Grow · Crown Dependencies Observatory</title>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/>
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@300;400;500;600;700;800&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;600&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/>
<script>
/* ── OBSERVATORY THEME SYNC ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Receives light/dark instructions from the parent App shell.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
(function () {
  if (window.__obsThemeReady) return;
  window.__obsThemeReady = true;
  function applyTheme(isLight) {
    if (document.body) document.body.classList.toggle('light', isLight);
  }
  window.addEventListener('message', function (e) {
    if (e.data && e.data.type === 'observatory-theme') {
      applyTheme(!!e.data.light);
    }
  });
  if (window.parent !== window) {
    window.parent.postMessage({ type: 'observatory-theme-request' }, '*');
  }
}());
</script>
<style>
/* observatory.css tokens — inlined for self-contained operation.
   Do not introduce new hex values; alias existing tokens instead. */
*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }

:root {
  --bg:       #0f172a;
  --surface:  #1e293b;
  --surface2: #162032;
  --border:   #1e293b;
  --border2:  #334155;
  --text:     #e2e8f0;
  --muted:    #64748b;
  --muted2:   #94a3b8;
  --blue:     #60a5fa;
  --blue-dim: #1d4ed8;
  --green:    #34d399;
  --amber:    #fbbf24;
  --red:      #f87171;   /* risk / error states only */
  --purple:   #a78bfa;
  --orange:   #fb923c;
  --iom: #3b82f6;
  --gsy: #10b981;
  --jsy: #f59e0b;
  --uk:  #94a3b8;
}

html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }

body {
  font-family: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif;
  background: var(--bg);
  color: var(--text);
  font-size: 14px;
  line-height: 1.5;
  -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
}

body.light {
  --bg: #f8fafc; --surface: #ffffff; --surface2: #f1f5f9;
  --border: #e2e8f0; --border2: #cbd5e1;
  --text: #0f172a; --muted: #64748b; --muted2: #475569;
  --blue: #2563eb; --blue-dim: rgba(37,99,235,0.08);
  --iom: #2563eb; --gsy: #059669; --jsy: #d97706; --uk: #64748b;
  --green: #059669; --amber: #d97706; --red: #dc2626;
  --purple: #7c3aed; --orange: #ea580c;
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after { transition: none !important; animation: none !important; }
  html { scroll-behavior: auto; }
}

.eyebrow {
  font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 10px;
}

input[type="range"] {
  -webkit-appearance: none; appearance: none; width: 100%; height: 3px;
  background: var(--border2); border-radius: 2px; outline: none; cursor: pointer;
}
input[type="range"]::-webkit-slider-thumb {
  -webkit-appearance: none; width: 14px; height: 14px; border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--blue); cursor: pointer;
}
input[type="range"]::-moz-range-thumb {
  width: 14px; height: 14px; border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--blue); cursor: pointer; border: none;
}

.pill {
  background: none; border: 1px solid var(--border2); border-radius: 20px;
  color: var(--muted2); cursor: pointer; font-family: inherit; font-size: 11px;
  font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: 0.03em; padding: 4px 12px;
  transition: color 0.15s, border-color 0.15s, background 0.15s; white-space: nowrap;
}
.pill:hover { border-color: var(--muted); color: var(--text); }

.legend { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 14px; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 11px; color: var(--muted2); }
.leg-item { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 5px; }
.leg-sw { width: 12px; height: 3px; border-radius: 1px; display: inline-block; flex-shrink: 0; }
.leg-dot { width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 50%; display: inline-block; flex-shrink: 0; }

/* ── Page layout ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.wrap { max-width: 1120px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 24px; }

.hero { min-height: 96vh; display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center; padding: 60px 0 30px; }
.hero h1 {
  font-size: clamp(34px, 5.5vw, 54px); font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  line-height: 1.08; color: var(--text); margin-bottom: 18px; max-width: 720px;
}
.hero .standfirst { font-size: clamp(15px, 1.8vw, 18px); color: var(--muted2); line-height: 1.7; max-width: 640px; margin-bottom: 34px; }
.hero .standfirst strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
.hero figure { max-width: 860px; }
.scroll-cue { margin-top: 26px; font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted); }
.scroll-cue::after { content: '↓'; margin-left: 8px; }

.act-head { padding: 15vh 0 6vh; max-width: 660px; }
.act-head .eyebrow { color: var(--blue); }
.act-head h2 { font-size: clamp(24px, 3.2vw, 34px); font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -0.015em; line-height: 1.15; }
.act-head .lede { margin-top: 12px; font-size: 15px; color: var(--muted2); line-height: 1.7; }

/* Within an act, the title pins to the top of the screen; the graphic pins
   below it (offset by the measured header height --hh); the text scrolls past. */
.act { padding-top: 12vh; }
.act .act-head {
  position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 6;
  background: var(--bg);            /* solid: scrolling text never shows through */
  padding: 12px 0 10px; max-width: none;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border2);
}
.act .act-head .eyebrow { margin-bottom: 4px; }
.act .act-head h2 { font-size: clamp(19px, 2.4vw, 25px); }
.act .act-head .lede { margin-top: 4px; font-size: 13px; max-width: 660px; }

.scene { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(260px, 340px) 1fr; gap: 44px; align-items: start; }
.g-col {
  position: sticky; top: var(--hh, 104px);
  height: calc(100vh - var(--hh, 104px));
  height: calc(100dvh - var(--hh, 104px));
  display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center; min-width: 0;
}
.steps { padding: 4vh 0 10vh; }
.step { min-height: 78vh; display: flex; align-items: center; }
.step:last-child { min-height: 60vh; }
.step-inner {
  border-left: 3px solid var(--border2); padding: 6px 0 6px 20px;
  font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--muted); transition: color 0.3s, border-color 0.3s;
}
.step.on .step-inner { border-left-color: var(--blue); color: var(--muted2); }
.step-inner strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
.step.on .keyline { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }

figure { min-width: 0; }
.fig-panel { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--border2); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 16px 10px; }
.fig-title { font-size: 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--text); margin-bottom: 2px; }
.fig-sub { font-size: 11px; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 10px; }
.fig-src { font-size: 10px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 8px; line-height: 1.5; }
svg { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; font-family: inherit; }
svg text { font-family: inherit; }

.fig-alt { display: none; }
.fig-alt.on { display: block; }

/* asides + tables */
details.aside, details.tbl {
  background: var(--surface2); border: 1px solid var(--border2); border-radius: 8px;
  padding: 10px 14px; margin-top: 14px; font-size: 12px; color: var(--muted2); line-height: 1.7;
}
details summary { cursor: pointer; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--muted); }
details[open] summary { margin-bottom: 8px; }
details.tbl table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: 11px; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
details.tbl th, details.tbl td { text-align: right; padding: 3px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border2); color: var(--muted2); white-space: nowrap; }
details.tbl th { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 600; }
details.tbl th:first-child, details.tbl td:first-child { text-align: left; }
details.tbl .tbl-scroll { max-height: 260px; overflow: auto; }

/* tooltip */
#tip {
  position: fixed; z-index: 50; pointer-events: none; display: none;
  background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--border2); border-radius: 7px;
  padding: 8px 11px; font-size: 11px; color: var(--muted2); line-height: 1.6;
  box-shadow: 0 6px 18px rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.45); min-width: 120px;
}
#tip .tip-yr { font-weight: 700; color: var(--text); margin-bottom: 3px; }
#tip .tip-row { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px; white-space: nowrap; }
#tip .tip-row i { width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 2px; flex-shrink: 0; }
#tip .tip-row b { margin-left: auto; color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; padding-left: 10px; }

/* loop diagram */
.loop-flow { stroke-dasharray: 5 7; }
.spin .loop-flow { animation: loopflow 1.6s linear infinite; }
.spin-fast .loop-flow { animation: loopflow 0.7s linear infinite; }
@keyframes loopflow { to { stroke-dashoffset: -48; } }
.loop-cut { opacity: 0; transition: opacity 0.5s; }
.loop-cut.on { opacity: 1; }

/* dumbbell rows */
.db-row { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(-8px); transition: opacity 0.5s, transform 0.5s; }
.db-row.on { opacity: 1; transform: none; }

/* annotations */
.ann { opacity: 0; transition: opacity 0.6s; }
.ann.on { opacity: 1; }

/* ── Simulator ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.sim { padding: 8vh 0 6vh; }
.sim-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 320px 1fr; gap: 24px; margin-top: 22px; align-items: start; }
.sim-controls { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--border2); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px; }
.slider-block { margin-bottom: 20px; }
.slider-block:last-of-type { margin-bottom: 12px; }
.slider-head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; margin-bottom: 7px; }
.slider-head label { font-size: 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--text); }
.slider-head output { font-size: 12px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--blue); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
.slider-hint { font-size: 10px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 6px; line-height: 1.5; }
.sim-readouts { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 14px; }
.card {
  background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--border2);
  border-top: 3px solid var(--border2); border-radius: 8px; padding: 13px 15px;
}
.card.c-iom { border-top-color: var(--iom); }
.card.c-amber { border-top-color: var(--amber); }
.card-label { font-size: 10px; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--muted2); margin-bottom: 5px; }
.card-value { font-size: 21px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1; color: var(--text); }
.card-sub { font-size: 11px; color: var(--muted2); margin-top: 4px; }
.sim-charts { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 14px; }
.sim-caveat { margin-top: 12px; font-size: 11px; color: var(--muted); font-style: italic; line-height: 1.6; }
.mini-stats { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 12px; }
.mini-stats .card { padding: 9px 12px; }
.mini-stats .card-value { font-size: 16px; }

/* ── Coda ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.coda { padding: 12vh 0 8vh; max-width: 640px; }
.coda p { font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted2); line-height: 1.9; margin-bottom: 16px; }
.coda a { color: var(--blue); text-decoration: none; }
.coda a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
.page-footer {
  margin-top: 32px; padding-top: 16px; border-top: 1px solid var(--border2);
  font-size: 10px; color: var(--muted); display: flex; justify-content: space-between; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px;
}
.page-footer a { color: var(--muted2); text-decoration: none; }
.page-footer a:hover { color: var(--text); }

/* ── Mobile (≤760px, both orientations): shared adjustments ───────────────── */
@media (max-width: 760px) {
  .wrap { padding: 0 16px; }
  .step-inner { font-size: 14px; }
  .hero { min-height: 0; padding-top: 48px; }
  .sim-grid, .sim-charts { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .sim-readouts, .mini-stats { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  /* touch-friendly sliders (Act 6 stacks and scrolls normally — never pinned) */
  input[type="range"] { height: 5px; }
  input[type="range"]::-webkit-slider-thumb { width: 22px; height: 22px; }
  input[type="range"]::-moz-range-thumb { width: 22px; height: 22px; }
}

/* ── Mobile portrait: first-class pinned layout ─────────────────────────────
   Compact one-line act title pinned at top; graphic pinned directly beneath;
   the pinned stack is capped at ~55% of the viewport (dvh with vh fallback,
   so address-bar collapse doesn't break the hold); step text scrolls in the
   space underneath and drives the graphic exactly as on desktop. */
@media (max-width: 760px) and (orientation: portrait) {
  .act { padding-top: 6vh; }
  .act .act-head {
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 7;
    background: var(--bg); padding: 8px 0 6px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border2);
  }
  .act .act-head .eyebrow, .act .act-head .lede { display: none; }
  .act .act-head h2 {
    font-size: 15px; white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  .scene { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0; }
  .g-col {
    grid-row: 1;
    position: sticky; top: var(--hh, 36px); z-index: 5;
    background: var(--bg);
    height: auto;
    max-height: calc(55vh - var(--hh, 36px));
    max-height: calc(55dvh - var(--hh, 36px));
    overflow: hidden;
    justify-content: flex-start;
    padding-bottom: 6px;
  }
  .steps { grid-row: 2; padding: 2vh 0 8vh; }
  .step { min-height: 46vh; min-height: 46dvh; margin: 0; display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .step:last-child { min-height: 40vh; min-height: 40dvh; }
  /* compact chart chrome at this size */
  .g-col .fig-panel { padding: 8px 10px 6px; border-radius: 8px; }
  .g-col .fig-title { font-size: 11px; margin-bottom: 0; }
  .g-col .fig-sub, .g-col .fig-src { display: none; }
  .g-col .legend { gap: 8px; font-size: 10px; margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .g-col .mini-stats { display: none; }
  /* data-table toggles are relocated below the steps by script; hide any
     stragglers so the pinned stack stays compact */
  .g-col details.tbl { display: none; }
  /* relocated tables auto-place into rows below the steps */
  .scene > details.tbl { margin-bottom: 6px; }
}

/* ── Small landscape (phone rotated): desktop pinned layout, scaled down ──── */
@media (min-width: 761px) and (max-height: 520px) {
  .act .act-head { padding: 7px 0 6px; }
  .act .act-head h2 { font-size: 16px; }
  .act .act-head .lede, .act .act-head .eyebrow { display: none; }
  .scene { gap: 22px; }
  .fig-panel { padding: 8px 10px 6px; }
  .fig-sub, .fig-src { display: none; }
  .g-col details.tbl { display: none; }
  .mini-stats { display: none; }
  .step { min-height: 92vh; }
  .step-inner { font-size: 13px; }
}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="wrap">

<!-- ═══════════════════════════ HERO ═══════════════════════════ -->
<header class="hero">
  <div class="eyebrow">Crown Dependencies Observatory · A data story</div>
  <h1>The Island That Didn't Grow</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Between 1998 and 2022 the United Kingdom grew by half.
  The Isle of Man — richer, nimbler, self-governing — grew by <strong>ten per cent</strong>.
  This is the story of why.</p>
  <figure class="fig-panel">
    <div class="fig-title">Real output since 1998</div>
    <div class="fig-sub">Gross value added, adjusted for prices. 1998 = 100.</div>
    <div class="legend"><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--iom)"></i>Isle of Man</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--uk)"></i>United Kingdom</span></div>
    <div id="hero-chart"></div>
    <div class="fig-src">Source: Isle of Man national income accounts; ONS UK national accounts. Chained volume measures, 2022 basis.</div>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll-cue">Scroll</div>
</header>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════ ACT 1 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<section class="act" id="act1">
<header class="act-head">
  <div class="eyebrow">Act 1</div>
  <h2>The pie that wouldn't grow</h2>
  <p class="lede">Two economies. The same weather. Very different voyages.</p>
</header>
<div class="scene">
  <div class="steps">
    <div class="step" data-act="a1" data-idx="0"><div class="step-inner">
      <strong>1998.</strong> Both economies are set to 100. Real output, with inflation stripped out. One line for the United Kingdom. One for the Isle of Man.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a1" data-idx="1"><div class="step-inner">
      The dotcom years were kind to both. By 2000 the Island stood at <strong>109</strong>, a point ahead of the UK. It would never be ahead again.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a1" data-idx="2"><div class="step-inner">
      <strong>2008.</strong> The banking crisis hit both. The UK fell six points and climbed back within four years. The Island fell and kept falling. By 2012 it was at <strong>92</strong> — below where it had started in 1998.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a1" data-idx="3"><div class="step-inner">
      <strong>2020.</strong> COVID cut roughly a tenth from each. Both bounced back within two years. The shocks were shared, year for year. The recoveries were not.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a1" data-idx="4"><div class="step-inner">
      By 2022 the UK stood at <strong>154</strong>. The Island stood at <strong>110</strong>.
      <p class="keyline" style="margin-top:12px">The same storms hit both ships. Only one made headway.</p>
      <div class="aside" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>Do you feel twice as rich as the UK?</strong> On paper you are: output per head runs at roughly &pound;71,000 here against &pound;38,000 there. But about three-fifths of the Island&rsquo;s output is company profit &mdash; banks, insurers, gaming operators &mdash; and most of it belongs to shareholders and clients who don&rsquo;t live here. It is booked here. It is not earned here. What residents take home is a different number, and this page is about that one.</div>
      <details class="aside"><summary>For the technically minded</summary>
      Both series are real gross value added: chained volume measures on a 2022 basis, UK from the ONS national accounts, Isle of Man from the Treasury's national income accounts. Indexing both to 1998 = 100 removes size and price level; only cumulative real growth remains. The Island's series is more volatile because one large sector can move the whole index. Base-year check: the gap is not an artefact of starting at 1998 — from every base year 1998&ndash;2012 the Island trails the UK to 2022 (by up to 44 points; from the 2000 peak, +1.3% in 22 years). The only flattering windows start in the 2012&ndash;13 trough, and rest entirely on the single 2022 observation (a one-year finance rebound, deflator-sensitive); measured 2013&ndash;2021 the UK leads again. Window-free: compound growth 0.4%/yr v 1.8%/yr.</details>
    </div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="g-col"><figure class="fig-panel">
    <div class="fig-title">Real output, Isle of Man v United Kingdom</div>
    <div class="fig-sub">Real GVA index, 1998 = 100</div>
    <div class="legend"><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--iom)"></i>Isle of Man</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--uk)"></i>United Kingdom</span></div>
    <div id="a1-chart"></div>
    <div class="fig-src">Source: Isle of Man national income accounts; ONS. Real GVA, 1998 = 100.</div>
    <details class="tbl"><summary>Data table</summary><div class="tbl-scroll" id="a1-tbl"></div></details>
  </figure></div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════ ACT 2 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<section class="act" id="act2">
<header class="act-head">
  <div class="eyebrow">Act 2</div>
  <h2>The churn</h2>
  <p class="lede">Inside the flat line of Act 1, everything was moving.</p>
</header>
<div class="scene">
  <div class="steps">
    <div class="step" data-act="a2" data-idx="0"><div class="step-inner">
      <strong>1997.</strong> Finance is two-fifths of the economy — banking, insurance, funds. Everything else shares the rest.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a2" data-idx="1"><div class="step-inner">
      Then come the flavours, each with its own promotion push. Film from 1995. <strong>E-gaming</strong> licensed from 2001; the big operators land from 2005. Space and digital follow. Watch the purple band.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a2" data-idx="2"><div class="step-inner">
      By 2016 e-gaming is <strong>28 per cent</strong> of the economy, from nothing in 1998. Meanwhile everything that is not finance, e-gaming or government has shrunk from 56 per cent of the economy to 26.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a2" data-idx="3"><div class="step-inner">
      Each new sector claimed a bigger slice. But hold Act 1 in mind: the total barely moved.
      <p class="keyline" style="margin-top:12px">The slices changed hands. The pie did not grow.</p>
      <details class="aside"><summary>For the technically minded</summary>
      Shares are of nominal GVA, so e-gaming's slice includes offshore operating margins booked to the Island; its share overstates its footprint in jobs and local spending. The step in the government band at 2007 is a reclassification in the accounts, not a hiring spree. Act 1, by contrast, measures real, price-adjusted output.</details>
    </div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="g-col"><figure class="fig-panel">
    <div class="fig-title">Who holds the slices</div>
    <div class="fig-sub">Share of nominal GVA, per cent, 1997–2023</div>
    <div class="legend"><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--blue)"></i>Finance</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--purple)"></i>E-gaming</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--green)"></i>Government</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--uk)"></i>Everything else</span></div>
    <div id="a2-chart"></div>
    <div class="fig-src">Source: Isle of Man national income accounts, sector shares of nominal GVA.</div>
    <details class="tbl"><summary>Data table</summary><div class="tbl-scroll" id="a2-tbl"></div></details>
  </figure></div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════ ACT 3 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<section class="act" id="act3">
<header class="act-head">
  <div class="eyebrow">Act 3</div>
  <h2>A fixed pool of people</h2>
  <p class="lede">New sectors need workers. The Island stopped producing them.</p>
</header>
<div class="scene">
  <div class="steps">
    <div class="step" data-act="a3" data-idx="0"><div class="step-inner">
      <strong>1951:</strong> 54,024 people. The Island then grew for sixty years — through the tourist trade, then the finance boom, then everything after. <strong>84,497</strong> by 2011.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a3" data-idx="1"><div class="step-inner">
      Then it stopped. 2011: 84,497. 2024: 84,756. Thirteen years, <strong>259 more people</strong>.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a3" data-idx="2"><div class="step-inner">
      Deaths have exceeded births every year since <strong>2017</strong>. In 2024 natural change was −351 and net migration +651. Every extra worker must now be imported, housed, and kept.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a3" data-idx="3"><div class="step-inner">
      So where did each new flavour find its workers? Not by retraining the old ones. <strong>The Island swaps people with the UK.</strong> Each new sector imports its specialists: older, experienced, mid-career. Meanwhile, between the 2001 and 2021 censuses: retail lost 1,314 jobs. Manufacturing lost 1,135. Farming and fishing lost 1,027. Hospitality lost 814. Finance itself lost 1,507.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a3" data-idx="4"><div class="step-inner">
      The workers those sectors no longer needed — disproportionately younger, local people — left. A flat population is not stillness. It is churn at equilibrium: specialists in, school-leavers out.
      <p class="keyline" style="margin-top:12px">On a full island, every migrant in is a migrant out.</p>
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a3" data-idx="5"><div class="step-inner">
      One employer grew: <strong>government</strong>. Health, education and administration added 2,643 jobs. And the biggest flavour barely hires. By the 2021 census — past the sector's peak share, which touched 30 per cent in 2017 — e-gaming was about a fifth of the economy on <strong>1,086 jobs</strong>, three per cent of the workforce.
      <details class="aside"><summary>For the technically minded</summary>
      Employment is from the census of population (residents in work, self-classified by industry), so it is a different lens from the GVA accounts. Total employment: 32,293 in 2001, 31,719 in 2021 — a twenty-year round trip. Note that a census measures stocks, not flows: it shows where jobs stood, not the gross traffic in between, so the two-way churn cannot be read from it directly. The churn mechanism — specialists in, displaced younger workers out — is the reading of the 2010/11 systems-dynamics work, consistent with these stocks. One honest limitation: the simulator in Act 6 carries net migration only; it cannot represent the two-way flow. E-gaming employment counts licence-holder staff; its £801m of GVA (2023) includes offshore margins.</details>
    </div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="g-col">
    <figure class="fig-panel fig-alt on" id="a3-figA">
      <div class="fig-title">Population of the Isle of Man</div>
      <div class="fig-sub">Residents, 1951–2024</div>
      <div class="legend"><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-dot" style="background:var(--iom)"></i>Census</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-dot" style="border:2px solid var(--iom);background:transparent"></i>Annual estimate</span></div>
      <div id="a3-pop"></div>
      <div class="fig-src">Source: Isle of Man census reports 1951–2021; annual population estimates (experimental) 2022–24.</div>
      <details class="tbl"><summary>Data table</summary><div class="tbl-scroll" id="a3-tblA"></div></details>
    </figure>
    <figure class="fig-panel fig-alt" id="a3-figB">
      <div class="fig-title">Where the jobs went, 2001 → 2021</div>
      <div class="fig-sub">Employment by sector, census counts</div>
      <div class="legend"><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-dot" style="background:var(--uk)"></i>2001 census</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-dot" style="background:var(--iom)"></i>2021 census</span></div>
      <div id="a3-db"></div>
      <div class="fig-src">Source: Isle of Man census of population, 2001 and 2021. Residents in employment by industry.</div>
      <details class="tbl"><summary>Data table</summary><div class="tbl-scroll" id="a3-tblB"></div></details>
    </figure>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════ ACT 4 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<section class="act" id="act4">
<header class="act-head">
  <div class="eyebrow">Act 4</div>
  <h2>The engine in the basement</h2>
  <p class="lede">If the economy didn't grow, what paid for the government that did?</p>
</header>
<div class="scene">
  <div class="steps">
    <div class="step" data-act="a4" data-idx="0"><div class="step-inner">
      The Island does not keep its own VAT takings directly. Under a revenue-sharing agreement with the UK it draws a share of a common pool. In 1998 that share brought in <strong>£156m</strong>. By 2007: <strong>£430m</strong>. It nearly trebled in nine years — while the economy, in real terms, went nowhere.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a4" data-idx="1"><div class="step-inner">
      This one stream rose from <strong>52 per cent to 72 per cent</strong> of Treasury receipts. Spending followed it up. So did public hiring. The loop on the right span faster each year: spend, get measured, claim the share, receive, spend again.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a4" data-idx="2"><div class="step-inner">
      <strong>2009 and 2011:</strong> the UK rebased the formula, twice. Receipts fell from £438m in 2006 to <strong>£296m</strong> in 2012. The engine had been paying for the government. Now the government had to shrink — and, mostly, didn't.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a4" data-idx="3"><div class="step-inner">
      <strong>2016:</strong> a new arrangement, FERSA, ties the share to the Island's own measured expenditure, averaged over five years. A further true-up follows in <strong>2025</strong>. Each revision moves the pool closer to what islanders actually spend.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a4" data-idx="4"><div class="step-inner">
      <p class="keyline">For a decade, the public sector's growth was paid for by a formula, not by the economy.</p>
      <details class="aside"><summary>For the technically minded</summary>
      Under the 1979 Customs and Excise Agreement, shared duties are pooled and divided by formula. Before 2009 the Island's share keyed off an arrangement that outgrew its measured economy; the 2009 and 2011 revisions and the 2016 Final Expenditure Revenue Sharing Arrangement progressively re-anchored it to actual island final expenditure, with a five-year averaging lag. Receipts here are nominal Customs &amp; Excise revenue, Treasury accounts.</details>
    </div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="g-col"><figure class="fig-panel">
    <div class="fig-title">Customs &amp; Excise receipts</div>
    <div class="fig-sub">£ million per year, nominal, 1998–2023</div>
    <div id="a4-chart"></div>
    <div id="a4-loop" style="margin-top:6px"></div>
    <div class="fig-src">Source: Isle of Man Treasury accounts, Customs &amp; Excise receipts.</div>
    <details class="tbl"><summary>Data table</summary><div class="tbl-scroll" id="a4-tbl"></div></details>
  </figure></div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════ ACT 5 ═══════════════════════════ -->
<section class="act" id="act5">
<header class="act-head">
  <div class="eyebrow">Act 5</div>
  <h2>Three claimants, one island</h2>
  <p class="lede">Put the three stories on one axis and the shape of the problem appears.</p>
</header>
<div class="scene">
  <div class="steps">
    <div class="step" data-act="a5" data-idx="0"><div class="step-inner">
      Take 2005 as 100. <strong>People:</strong> plus seven per cent in twenty years. <strong>Output:</strong> plus twelve. Two nearly flat lines.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a5" data-idx="1"><div class="step-inner">
      Now add houses. The median home sold for £175,000 in 2005 and <strong>£310,000</strong> in 2025. Plus 77 per cent, on a population that barely moved.
    </div></div>
    <div class="step" data-act="a5" data-idx="2"><div class="step-inner">
      Three claimants bid for one fixed pool of workers and homes: the <strong>public sector</strong>, the <strong>existing economy</strong>, and <strong>this year's flavour</strong>. A new sector does not conjure new people. It outbids an old employer for staff, and outbids a neighbour for a house.
      <p class="keyline" style="margin-top:12px">Our competition isn't with the globe. It's internal.</p>
      <details class="aside"><summary>For the technically minded</summary>
      House prices are median transaction prices, nominal; the series has a basis change at 2002, so 2005 is used as the common base year. Population between censuses is interpolated linearly. Output is the real GVA index of Act 1, rebased to 2005 (latest year 2022). In a supply-constrained labour and housing market, sector promotion largely reallocates inputs and capitalises into prices — the mechanism the simulator below makes explicit.</details>
    </div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="g-col"><figure class="fig-panel">
    <div class="fig-title">People, output, house prices</div>
    <div class="fig-sub">Indexed, 2005 = 100</div>
    <div class="mini-stats">
      <div class="card"><div class="card-label">Population</div><div class="card-value">+7%</div><div class="card-sub">2005–2024</div></div>
      <div class="card c-iom"><div class="card-label">Real output</div><div class="card-value">+12%</div><div class="card-sub">2005–2022</div></div>
      <div class="card c-amber"><div class="card-label">Median house</div><div class="card-value">+77%</div><div class="card-sub">2005–2025</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="legend"><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--amber)"></i>Median house price</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--iom)"></i>Real output</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--green)"></i>Population</span></div>
    <div id="a5-chart"></div>
    <div class="fig-src">Source: Isle of Man house price statistics (median transaction price); national income accounts; census reports. All indexed, 2005 = 100.</div>
    <details class="tbl"><summary>Data table</summary><div class="tbl-scroll" id="a5-tbl"></div></details>
  </figure></div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════ ACT 6 · SIMULATOR ═══════════════════════════ -->
<section class="sim" id="act6">
  <div class="act-head" style="padding-bottom:0">
    <div class="eyebrow">Act 6</div>
    <h2>Run the Island</h2>
    <p class="lede">This page rests on a small calibrated model: three private sectors, a public sector,
    houses, people, and the VAT formula. It reproduces the history you have just scrolled through.
    From 2025, the controls are yours. Runs extend to 2040.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="sim-grid">
    <div class="sim-controls">
      <div class="slider-block">
        <div class="slider-head"><label for="sl-promo">Promotion intensity</label><output id="out-promo">25%</output></div>
        <input type="range" id="sl-promo" min="0" max="100" value="25" step="5" aria-describedby="hint-promo"/>
        <div class="slider-hint" id="hint-promo">How hard the Island courts the next sector. 25% is roughly today's tax-capped level; 100% is the 2005–15 push.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="slider-block">
        <div class="slider-head"><label for="sl-hire">Public-sector hiring restraint</label><output id="out-hire">0%</output></div>
        <input type="range" id="sl-hire" min="0" max="100" value="0" step="5" aria-describedby="hint-hire"/>
        <div class="slider-hint" id="hint-hire">0% hires as revenue allows, as history did. 100% freezes headcount.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="slider-block">
        <div class="slider-head"><label for="sl-house">Housing completions</label><output id="out-house">100%</output></div>
        <input type="range" id="sl-house" min="50" max="300" value="100" step="10" aria-describedby="hint-house"/>
        <div class="slider-hint" id="hint-house">New homes per year, relative to the recent rate of roughly 300.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="slider-block">
        <div class="slider-head"><label for="sl-mig">Migration openness</label><output id="out-mig">100%</output></div>
        <input type="range" id="sl-mig" min="0" max="200" value="100" step="10" aria-describedby="hint-mig"/>
        <div class="slider-hint" id="hint-mig">How freely workers move in response to jobs. 100% matches calibrated history; 0% closes the door.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="slider-block">
        <div class="slider-head"><label for="sl-world">World growth</label><output id="out-world">1.4%/yr</output></div>
        <input type="range" id="sl-world" min="0" max="3" value="1.4" step="0.1" aria-describedby="hint-world"/>
        <div class="slider-hint" id="hint-world">The model's demand input is UK growth — the observed path through history. This sets its future from 2025. 1.4%/yr is the baseline assumption.</div>
      </div>
      <button class="pill" id="sim-reset" type="button">Reset to baseline</button>
      <div class="sim-caveat">A model, not a forecast. It is the story above made mechanical. Use it to compare policies, not to predict 2040. Controls apply from 2025; history to 2024 is fixed.</div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="sim-readouts">
        <div class="card c-iom"><div class="card-label">Real output 2040</div><div class="card-value" id="ro-gva">–</div><div class="card-sub" id="ro-gva-sub">index, 1998 = 100</div></div>
        <div class="card"><div class="card-label">Population 2040</div><div class="card-value" id="ro-pop">–</div><div class="card-sub" id="ro-pop-sub">residents</div></div>
        <div class="card c-amber"><div class="card-label">Median house 2040</div><div class="card-value" id="ro-hp">–</div><div class="card-sub" id="ro-hp-sub">nominal</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sim-charts">
        <div class="fig-panel">
          <div class="fig-title">Real output</div>
          <div class="fig-sub">Real GVA index, 1998 = 100</div>
          <div class="legend"><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--iom)"></i>Your run</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--muted);height:0;border-top:2px dashed var(--muted)"></i>Baseline model</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-dot" style="background:var(--uk)"></i>History (observed)</span></div>
          <div id="sim-gva"></div>
          <div class="fig-src">Observed: Isle of Man national income accounts to 2022.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fig-panel">
          <div class="fig-title">House prices</div>
          <div class="fig-sub">Median transaction price, £ thousand, nominal</div>
          <div class="legend"><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--amber)"></i>Your run</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--muted);height:0;border-top:2px dashed var(--muted)"></i>Baseline model</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-dot" style="background:var(--uk)"></i>History (observed)</span></div>
          <div id="sim-hp"></div>
          <div class="fig-src">Observed: Isle of Man house price statistics to 2025.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fig-panel" style="grid-column:1 / -1">
          <div class="fig-title">Sectors under your run</div>
          <div class="fig-sub">Real GVA by sector, £ million, 2022 prices, 1998–2040</div>
          <div class="legend"><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--blue)"></i>Finance</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--purple)"></i>E-gaming</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--uk)"></i>Existing economy</span><span class="leg-item"><i class="leg-sw" style="background:var(--green)"></i>Public sector</span></div>
          <div id="sim-sec"></div>
          <div class="fig-src">Model output; sector definitions as in Act 2. Public sector is GVA of government services.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <details class="tbl" style="margin-top:12px"><summary>Data table — your run v baseline</summary><div class="tbl-scroll" id="sim-tbl"></div></details>
      <details class="aside"><summary>For the technically minded</summary>
      The simulator is a line-for-line JavaScript port of the calibrated pass-2 systems-dynamics model (annual deterministic loop, 1998–2040): sectors for finance, e-gaming and the existing economy; migration responding to vacancy pressure; a relative-cost index that crowds out the existing sector; house prices driven by the people-to-dwellings ratio; and the customs formula in its three historical regimes. Port parity against the Python original is exact to floating-point rounding (see source comment). Beyond 2026, external inputs are held steady: UK growth 1.4%/yr, inflation 2%, interest rates 3.5%. The world-growth lever replaces the UK real-growth input from 2025 onward (history keeps the observed path) and also scales the e-gaming global demand ceiling by 15 per cent per percentage point of growth above or below the 1.4% baseline; at its default the lever reproduces the baseline path exactly.</details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════ CODA ═══════════════════════════ -->
<footer class="coda">
  <p>The analysis behind this page was first set out in a 2010 systems-dynamics study and a
  2011 discussion paper delivered to the Isle of Man Treasury. Every number is from official
  published sources.</p>
  <p><a href="economy-explorer.html">Explore the data yourself in the Economy Explorer →</a></p>
  <details class="aside"><summary>Data sources</summary>
    <ul style="padding-left:18px">
      <li>Isle of Man Government, national income accounts — GVA in real terms, constant 2022 values, and nominal sector shares</li>
      <li>Office for National Statistics — UK GVA, chained volume measures</li>
      <li>Isle of Man census reports, 1951–2021; annual population estimates (experimental), 2022–24</li>
      <li>Isle of Man census of population — employment by industry, 2001 and 2021</li>
      <li>Isle of Man Treasury — Customs &amp; Excise receipts, annual accounts</li>
      <li>Isle of Man Government — house price statistics, median transaction price</li>
    </ul>
  </details>
